<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colosseum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">72–80</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Flavian Amphitheatre, Amphitheatrum Flavium, Colosseo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Colosseum is an elliptical amphitheatre in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, just east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Roman Forum</a>, and the largest ancient amphitheatre ever built. Construction began under Emperor Vespasian in 72 and was completed in 80 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a>, with further changes under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>. Built of travertine, tuff and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-concrete">Roman concrete</a>, it held an estimated 50,000 to 80,000 spectators for gladiatorial contests, animal hunts, executions and other spectacles. It ceased to be used for entertainment in the early medieval era and was later used for housing, workshops, a fortress and a quarry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colosseum.jpg" alt="Colosseum" width="330" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FeaturedPics · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colosseo_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His Flavian dynasty restored stability and began the <b>Colosseum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Standing between the <b>Colosseum</b> and the Palatine Hill, it is the largest Roman triumphal arch.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-25">25 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
